"## Identification of the missing file\n\n**Exhaustive inventory confirms the workspace contains exactly one file:** `.gitignore` (containing only `/.kuma/`). There is no git repository, no source code, no documentation, and no metadata of any kind.\n\n### The missing file: the release checklist itself\n\n**Primary missing file:** the release checklist document (e.g. `release_checklist.md`, `CHECKLIST.md`, or similar).\n\nThis is the file whose absence blocks the original checklist, and here is the causal chain:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Teammate reports: checklist cannot be completed] --> B{Locate release checklist file}\n    B --> C[File does not exist in workspace]\n    C --> D[Cannot enumerate required items]\n    D --> E[Cannot execute any checklist step]\n    E --> F[Release process fully blocked]\n```\n\n**How this blocks the original checklist:**\n\n1. **No items can be enumerated.** A checklist is only executable if its steps are written down. With the document absent, nobody can know whether it requires a changelog update, a version bump, tag creation, artifact verification, or sign-off.\n2. **No verification commands can be run.** Release checklists typically contain runnable checks (`make test`, lint, build). None of the underlying project files exist, so even reconstructed steps would fail immediately.\n3. **No state to check against.** The checklist's purpose is to compare current repo state against release requirements. The workspace has no repo state at all (`git status` fails: *not a git repository*).\n4. **Compounding secondary absences.** Because the workspace is empty, any file the checklist referenced is also missing: changelog, version manifest, source, release notes, build outputs. The checklist document is the root blocker; these are the downstream casualties.\n\n### Honest caveat\n\nWithout the original document, I cannot name a more specific missing artifact (like \"CHANGELOG.md\") with evidence. The only verifiable claim is: **the